<header class="page-head page-head--story">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The story</p>
    <h1 class="page-head__title">It started with cards <em>nobody else wanted.</em></h1>
    <p class="page-head__lede">No company invented Canadian Highlander. No designer pitched it. It grew in one city on the edge of the continent, at one comic shop, among friends who refused to stop playing their old cards.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="wrap">
  <ol class="chapters">
    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>1999</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Prologue · Victoria, British Columbia</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">An island of eternal players.</h2>
        <p>Victoria sits at the southern tip of Vancouver Island, a ferry ride from anywhere. Its Magic scene has always skewed old school: people who held on to their collections while the rest of the world chased the next Standard. In 1999 a small group of them started building hundred-card piles out of the cards they loved most. Pet cards. Cards that weren't good enough for Extended. Cards that had nowhere else to go.</p>
        <p>There was no points list yet. There was barely a name. There was just a table, a stack of singletons each, and a question every Magic player eventually asks: <em>what if I could play everything I own?</em></p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Tolarian Academy", null, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2000s</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter one</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">The quiet years.</h2>
        <p>Then people left. School, work, life on the mainland. The format nearly faded out with them, a house rule remembered by a few. But Victoria has a way of pulling people back, and as players returned they brought the game with them and taught it to whoever would sit down. A few dedicated evangelists kept it alive long enough to need something it had never had: structure.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2007</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter two · Yellowjacket Comics &amp; Toys</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">The first tournament, and the first problem.</h2>
        <p>In 2007 the format held its first real tournament, at Yellowjacket Comics in Victoria: the store the community still calls its birthplace. Tournaments meant prizes, prizes meant people played to win, and playing to win meant somebody was going to find the most broken thing in a card pool with no restrictions at all.</p>
        <p>One of the first was Tinker. Play an artifact, Tinker it into Darksteel Colossus, and the game was effectively over before the other player had done anything that mattered. A casual format had met its first degenerate deck, and it needed an answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Tinker", null, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2008</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter three</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Don't ban it. Price it.</h2>
        <p>About a year later the community did what every format does and wrote a ban list. It never sat right. Banning cards meant telling people that the Black Lotus in their binder, the reason many of them still played, was off the table. So the ban list slowly turned into something else.</p>
        <p>Instead of cutting the most powerful cards, Victoria taxed them. Each one got a price. Every deck got the same small budget, seven points by the early 2010s, to spend however it liked. Nothing had to be banned for being too good. You could have Lotus. You just couldn't have Lotus <em>and</em> everything else. It is still the best idea in the format.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Black Lotus", 7, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2014</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter four · "Highlander for Staples"</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Twenty-eight players and a Mana Drain.</h2>
        <p>By August 2014 the Yellowjacket events were drawing a crowd. One of them, played for a Mana Drain, pulled in 28 players, from regulars to once-in-a-blue-moon locals. The winner: Benjamin Wheeler, on mono-green Craterhoof, spending seven points across a hundred cards. In the top four, Robin Sorensen with a blue-green tempo deck people had already started calling "UG Sorensen."</p>
        <p>Remember those two names. Twelve years later, both of them sit on the council that sets the points list.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Mana Drain", 1, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2016</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter five</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">The world finds out.</h2>
        <p>Marshall Sutcliffe of the Limited Resources podcast learned the format from Victoria players and wrote it up in 2015, thanking the locals who'd taught him and kept the list. On New Year's Day 2016 the budget rose from seven points to ten, with the biggest cards repriced to match. That May, Wizards of the Coast published Sutcliffe's introduction to Canadian Highlander on its own site, calling it the most fun Constructed format he'd ever played.</p>
        <p>Suddenly a format that had lived inside one comic shop had readers on every continent.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Time Walk", 5, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip chapter--noart">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2017</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter six</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Victoria gets a broadcast.</h2>
        <p>LoadingReadyRun, the Victoria comedy and gaming crew, launched North 100: a Canadian Highlander podcast covering set reviews, brewing, and points changes. For a lot of players outside BC, it was the first time they heard the format explained by people who played it every week.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--noart">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>Online</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter seven</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">The table gets bigger.</h2>
        <p>Then the table went online. The Discord became the format's town square, with weekly tournaments on webcam and Cockatrice, monthly leagues that include Magic Online, and pickup games at all hours. Players who'd never been to Vancouver Island were now testing decks, writing primers and organizing events of their own. The format that began among a few friends had become a community that never really logs off.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2026</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter eight</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Seven seats at the table.</h2>
        <p>In January 2026 the council did something new: it grew from five seats to seven and voted in two members from the Discord and international community, Cyclopes8 and Kelvin. For the first time, the people setting the points list reached beyond the city where it started.</p>
        <p>The year kept moving. In March, Time Walk and Thassa's Oracle got cheaper while The One Ring and Tamiyo picked up a point each. In September, a combo-heavy metagame led the council to price Enlightened Tutor, Spellseeker and The Fantasticar, and to push Tolarian Academy to two. That same month a Victoria regular, Trenton M., took the newest seat.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Enlightened Tutor", 1, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <section class="epilogue">
    <p class="eyebrow">Epilogue</p>
    <h2 class="h2">The next chapter opens <em>{{ site.nextPointsUpdate | longDate }}.</em></h2>
    <p>Twenty-seven years on, the format still runs on the same idea: every card is legal, every deck gets the same budget, and the people who play it decide what things cost. The next points window is weeks away. The next weekly is this Saturday. There's a seat.</p>
